  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 502 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe long term forecast period opens in transition, with the \nantecedent longwave troughing over the Eastern CONUS propagating off \nto the east as amplified ridging sets up upstream in the Great \nPlains and Upper Midwest. The beginning of the period is marked by \nthe passage of the last few shortwave impulses around the backside \nof the aforementioned trough. These disturbances will keep some \nclouds in the sky on Sunday night, but a clearing trend is \nanticipated through Monday as the influence of that ridging grows. \nThe forecast guidance suite collectively resolves the mid/upper \nlevel ridge axis immediately upstream of the CWA at the start of the \nnew work week. This feature is progged to increase in \nmagnitude/amplitude through midweek, with LREF mean 500mb \ngeopotential heights rising towards the 95th percentile of model \nclimatology by Thursday. The increasing synoptic scale subsidence \nassociated with this strengthening ridge will feed the attendant \nsurface high pressure system over the forecast area and yield a very \ndry airmass throughout the atmospheric column. Collectively, these \nsynoptics suggest that efficient diurnal processes will govern the \ndaily sensible weather conditions across Eastern Kentucky next week. \nModel spread increases once the ridging gets suppressed and moves \neast-southeast on Friday, but before then, confidence is quite high \nthat the JKL CWA will experience fair skies, seasonably chilly \nnights, and a gradual warming trend. \n\nThe most notable deviations from the baseline NBM guidance used to \npopulate the long term forecast grids will come in the form of \novernight ridge-valley temperature splits. Light pressure gradient \nwinds and mostly clear skies should easily allow the valleys to \ndecouple after sunset each night. As the colder air pools into \nthese lower elevation locales on Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, and \nWednesday nights, temperatures should easily drop down below the \nfreezing mark. While ridgetops will likely remain near to just \nabove the freezing point, the conventionally- coldest sheltered \nand shaded hollows could drop down into the lower half of the 20s\nin this time frame. This would mark the coldest temperatures of \nthe season thus far. NBM ensemble probabilities for MinTs less \nthan or equal to 25 degrees peak in the 60-90% range on Monday \nnight/Tuesday morning. This coincides with the surface high's \nclosest proximity, which makes sense given that the ridging's \neastward shift should yield a warming trend later on in the \nperiod. High temperatures should gradually moderate from \nwidespread highs in the 50s on Monday to the upper 60s/lower 70s \non Thursday and Friday. It will intrinsically become harder for \nlows to get quite as cold as they did earlier in the work week, \nbut lows should remain below freezing through Thursday morning. \nIncreasing mid/upper level cloud cover late in the period could \ninsulate overnight lows above freezing, but this warming trend is \nnot accompanied by meaningful low level moisture return. Expect \ndewpoints to remain in the 20s and 30s through the end of the \nperiod. This, in turn, will keep precipitation chances below \nmentionable values through the end of the forecast period. So, \naside from some potential frost-related window-scraping on the \nchilliest mornings, very little weather-related societal impacts \nare anticipated across Eastern Kentucky between Monday and Friday.",
